        <div id="set-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Set Time Is the Whole Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every product on this list sets in minutes. DRYLOK publishes 3 to 5 minutes, Quikrete Hydraulic Water-Stop a final set of 5 minutes or less, Sakrete Leak Stopper an initial set of 1 to 3 minutes, and Quikrete's FastSet claims to stop flowing water in 60 to 90 seconds. Those figures are measured under lab conditions, typically around room temperature. In practice your working window is shorter than the set time, and the manufacturers say so: DRYLOK advises mixing only what you can place in about three minutes, and Quikrete says two to three.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature changes everything. Quikrete's data sheet notes that hot temperatures reduce set time and recommends warm water below 50°F. A batch mixed with warm water in a warm basement in August can stiffen in a couple of minutes. Cool water helps in summer, and the material should not go on when the substrate is colder than the minimum on the label, typically around 40°F.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule is small batches. Mix a golf-ball to tennis-ball sized amount, place it, and mix again. Never add water to a batch that has started to stiffen; the set has begun, and remixing breaks the structure that is forming, leaving a plug that looks fine and crumbles later.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need more working time, Xypex Patch'n Plug's final set of up to nine minutes gives a little more room, while the FastSet and Rapid Set products give less. Choose based on the leak: slow seeps reward a slightly longer window, while a running hole needs the fastest product you can handle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Preparing the Crack So the Plug Holds
          </h2>

          <p>
            A hydraulic cement plug is only as good as the opening it locks into. Smearing cement over the face of a hairline crack does almost nothing, because there is nothing for the expansion to grip. The crack needs to be opened up first, typically to around three quarters of an inch wide and deep, with a cold chisel or a grinder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Undercut it. The channel should be wider at the back than at the face, an inverted V or dovetail, so the expanding cement keys in and cannot be pushed back out by water pressure. Clean out every bit of loose material, dust and old paint, because hydraulic cement bonds to sound concrete, not to debris.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dampen the surface before placing the cement on a dry crack, so the dry concrete does not suck water out of the mix. On an actively leaking crack, the water is already there. Work from the top of a vertical crack downward, or from the driest end toward the wettest, and leave the point where the water flows strongest until last.
          </p>

          <p>
            Press the cement in hard and hold it. Many data sheets describe forming the material into a plug and holding it in place by hand until it sets, and Sakrete describes pushing material from a plastic bag into underwater leaks. Holding pressure for the full set time is what turns a lump of cement into a plug that stays.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-not" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When Hydraulic Cement Is the Wrong Fix
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hydraulic cement is the right fix for an active leak through a hole, a tie-rod opening, a pipe penetration, or a non-moving crack in a concrete or block wall. It is the wrong fix for several common basement problems, and using it in those cases wastes the afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moving cracks need flexibility. Settlement cracks that change width with the seasons, or horizontal cracks in block walls that suggest the wall is bowing, are not leaks to be plugged but symptoms to be assessed. Horizontal cracks and walls that bulge inward are structural warning signs and deserve a professional look.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water at the joint between floor and wall, the cove joint, is often hydrostatic pressure pushing water under the wall rather than through it. Plugging the joint can simply move the water somewhere else. Our guide to <a href="/water-coming-through-basement-floor/">water coming through a basement floor</a> covers why, and interior or exterior drainage is often the lasting answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            General dampness, efflorescence and a damp smell without visible leaks are usually a coating and humidity problem rather than a plugging problem. A masonry waterproofer and a properly sized dehumidifier do more there than any amount of hydraulic cement, and our <a href="/basement-moisture-control/">basement moisture control guide</a> walks through the options.
          </p>

          <p>
            A small number of jobs suit a slower, stronger patching mortar rather than hydraulic cement at all. Large spalled areas, broken corners and shallow surface damage that is not leaking are easier to repair with a conventional repair mortar or vinyl concrete patch, which gives you time to shape and feather the edges. Hydraulic cement's speed is a liability there, and it is not meant to be feathered thin. Keep it for openings that are wet or leaking, where the fast set and slight expansion are exactly what the job needs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. After the Plug: Coating and Finishing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once the plug has set, you can usually shave it flush and move on. Many products can be coated soon after: DRYLOK says Fast Plug can be painted with its waterproofers once set, and Sika's material says Waterplug can take an appropriate topcoat after about 15 minutes. Check the specific waterproofer's instructions for cure time before coating, because some want the patch fully cured.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coating the whole wall after plugging is good practice. The plug stops the leak you can see; a masonry waterproofer reduces the general seepage through the rest of the wall. Applying both in the same weekend is a common and sensible basement repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep an eye on the repair through the next heavy rain or snowmelt. A plug that stays dry has done its job. A plug with water running around its edges usually means the crack was not undercut enough or the water found a path around it, and the answer is to cut it out and redo it rather than layer more cement over the top.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are finishing the basement afterwards, wait until the walls and floor have stayed dry through a wet season. A finished wall or floor hides leaks until they become mold problems, which is why our <a href="/best-flooring-for-basement/">basement flooring guide</a> puts fixing water entry first, before any flooring decision.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Choosing a Brand and Package Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a single leaking hole or a few short cracks, a 4 to 10 pound tub is plenty. DRYLOK gives coverage of about 16 cubic inches per pound, so even a 4 pound tub fills a meaningful length of three-quarter-inch channel, and a partly used tub keeps if sealed and stored dry; DRYLOK lists a three-year shelf life.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a whole foundation with many tie-rod holes and cracks, or for contractors, a 20 to 50 pound pail is better value per pound. Quikrete, DRYLOK and Sika all sell 50 pound sizes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Between the big home-center brands, the differences are small. DRYLOK Fast Plug and Quikrete Hydraulic Water-Stop perform similarly on paper, and buying whichever is in stock is a reasonable strategy. Choose Quikrete FastSet for running water, Sakrete Leak Stopper if that is what your store carries, and the pro products when you need their specific strengths.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, wear gloves and eye protection. Cement is alkaline and irritates skin and eyes, and the dry powders contain crystalline silica, so mix in a ventilated space and wear a dust mask when pouring powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store unused powder sealed and off the floor. Moisture in a damp basement can start to hydrate the cement in an opened tub, leaving hard lumps that will not mix or set properly. If the powder no longer flows freely, buy a fresh tub; a few dollars of new material is cheaper than redoing a failed plug.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing Too Much at Once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hydraulic cement gives you two to three minutes of working time. A big batch sets in the bucket before you can place it. Mix a golf-ball to tennis-ball sized amount, place it, and mix again until the job is done.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Undercutting the Crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Smearing cement over a hairline crack gives the expansion nothing to grip, and water pushes the patch out. Open the crack to about three quarters of an inch and undercut it so the back is wider than the face.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Re-tempering a Stiffening Batch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Adding water to cement that has started to set breaks the structure that is forming. The patch may look fine and crumble weeks later. Throw it out and mix a fresh, smaller batch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging a Moving or Structural Crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rigid hydraulic cement cannot flex with a crack that opens and closes, and it does nothing about a wall that is bowing. Horizontal cracks and bulging walls need a structural assessment, not a plug.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the Plug as a Waterproof Wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hydraulic cement stops individual leaks; it does not waterproof the rest of the wall. Follow up with a masonry waterproofer and fix drainage, or the water will find the next weak spot.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best hydraulic cement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most basement leaks, DRYLOK Fast Plug is the best all-round choice, with a published 3 to 5 minute set time, a clear strength curve and sizes from 4 to 50 pounds. Quikrete Hydraulic Water-Stop performs similarly on paper and is a good alternative if that is what your store stocks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does hydraulic cement take to set?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most set in about three to five minutes. DRYLOK lists 3 to 5 minutes, Quikrete a final set of 5 minutes or less, and Sakrete an initial set of 1 to 3 minutes. Warm water and warm rooms shorten the set, and your real working window is usually only two to three minutes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can hydraulic cement stop running water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, that is what it is designed for. For water actively running out of a hole, a faster product such as Quikrete FastSet Water-Stop, which Quikrete says stops flowing water in 60 to 90 seconds, is easier to use. Form a plug, press it in and hold it until it sets.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is hydraulic cement waterproof?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The set cement is dense and seals the opening it fills, but it is a plug, not a waterproof coating. It stops a leak through a specific hole or crack. For general seepage through a wall, apply a masonry waterproofer over the wall after plugging.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use hydraulic cement on a basement floor crack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for non-moving cracks that leak. Undercut the crack, clean it, and pack the cement in. Water coming up at the joint between the floor and wall is often a drainage or pressure problem, though, and plugging it may just move the water elsewhere.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over hydraulic cement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes. DRYLOK says Fast Plug can be painted with its waterproofers once set, and Sika says Waterplug can be topcoated after about 15 minutes. Check the coating's own instructions for cure time before painting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a hydraulic cement repair last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A properly undercut plug in a non-moving crack can last for many years. Plugs fail early when the crack was not undercut, the batch was re-tempered, or the crack is still moving. If water runs around the plug after the next storm, cut it out and redo it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

